/* ============================================================
   MARK-LOSS ANALYSIS  (original content — O Level Pakistan)
   ------------------------------------------------------------
   Our own conclusions, written in our own words, from working
   through every 5054 Physics Paper 2 from 2023 to 2026 and
   studying how marks are awarded and lost on each question.
   This is teaching advice we wrote. It is not reproduced from
   any Cambridge publication.
   ============================================================ */

const EXAMINER = {
physics: {
  source: "our analysis of 14 Physics Paper 2s, 2023\u20132026",
  keyMessages: [
    "Read the whole question before starting, so your answer does not accidentally answer a later part.",
    "For calculations: write the formula first, then substitute the numbers, then convert any prefixes. Examiners award marks for the method even when the final answer is wrong.",
    "Give answers to at least the number of significant figures in the question data — usually two. Fractions are not accepted as final answers.",
    "If a question says 'explain', you must apply a physical principle. Simply stating the answer earns nothing.",
    "Always give the unit when it is not printed on the answer line. Marks are lost for missing or wrong units.",
    "The marks available and the space provided tell you how long the answer should be. Writing past the space usually means you are adding irrelevant detail.",
    "When a diagram is asked for or suggested, draw it carefully with a ruler and label it. A good diagram often communicates better than a paragraph.",
    "Avoid stock phrases like 'to avoid parallax error' or 'to make it more accurate' unless you say exactly how, in that specific experiment.",
    "Use g = 9.8 m/s² from the front cover unless the question gives a different value.",
    "Answer the question actually asked — not a similar one you have seen in a past paper."
  ],
  topicNotes: {
    0: [
      "Distance is the AREA under a speed–time graph, or average speed × time. Examiners report most students know distance = speed × time but forget it must be the AVERAGE speed.",
      "When objects stick together after a collision, use the COMBINED mass afterwards. Examiners flag students using only one vehicle's mass.",
      "Many students confuse 'momentum' with 'moment of a force'. They are completely different — know both definitions cold.",
      "The unit of momentum is kg m/s (or N s). Examiners saw many wrong versions like kg/m/s or kg m/s².",
      "For work done, you must say the distance moved is IN THE DIRECTION OF THE FORCE. Only a minority included this.",
      "For moments, identify which of the given distances is the PERPENDICULAR one. Wrong distance means lost marks even with the right formula.",
      "Convert grams to kilograms before finding weight. Examiners repeatedly saw answers 1000× too large.",
      "A kilowatt-hour is a unit of ENERGY: the energy transferred in 1 hour at a power of 1 kW. Most students did not know this."
    ],
    1: [
      "Converting between °C and kelvin was known only by stronger candidates. 0 °C = 273 K, so 100 °C = 373 K. Practise both directions.",
      "When explaining gas pressure changes, say collisions with the walls are MORE (or LESS) FREQUENT. Examiners explicitly reject 'the particles have more space to move' on its own.",
      "For melting or boiling, say energy is used to BREAK BONDS or separate particles — that is why the temperature stays constant.",
      "'More vibration' is too vague. Say the vibrations have larger amplitude or are more vigorous.",
      "In specific heat calculations, use the temperature CHANGE, not the final temperature. Examiners report this as a very common error.",
      "In convection answers, say the heated fluid becomes LESS DENSE and therefore rises. Few students mention density.",
      "Heat reaching water from a heater in contact with it is CONDUCTION, not convection. Many students confuse the two in the same question."
    ],
    2: [
      "When drawing diffraction, the wavelength must NOT change through the gap. Examiners specifically flag drawings where spacing widens after the gap.",
      "Diffracted crests should be semicircular, centred on the middle of the gap.",
      "Define wavelength as the distance between two successive crests, or between one point and the next identical point.",
      "When a wave enters a new medium the FREQUENCY stays the same; speed and wavelength change. Reuse that same frequency in your calculation.",
      "For a virtual image: rays diverge and only appear to meet when traced back, so it cannot be caught on a screen.",
      "Say the display is BETWEEN the lens and the principal focus. Vague phrases like 'before the focal length' earn nothing.",
      "Frequency must be defined as waves per unit time, or per second. 'In a certain time' or 'in a given time' scores zero.",
      "From a parallel-sided block, the emergent ray must be drawn PARALLEL to the incident ray.",
      "The property of ultraviolet that causes damage is that it is IONISING. Examiners say this was rarely stated."
    ],
    3: [
      "In a series circuit, use the TOTAL resistance to find current. Examiners report most students wrongly put the full e.m.f. across just one resistor.",
      "Ohm's law is current proportional to voltage AT CONSTANT TEMPERATURE. That last condition is a mark and is often missed.",
      "Cells in PARALLEL do not add voltage. Two 1.5 V cells in parallel still give 1.5 V — this confused many students.",
      "On an oscilloscope, multiply the trace height by the Y-gain to get voltage. Examiners report most students forgot to multiply at all.",
      "Watch the timebase units. If the timebase is in milliseconds, convert before finding frequency — almost all students were out by 10³.",
      "A thermistor's resistance FALLS as temperature rises. Many students misremember this and lose an entire part.",
      "For transformers, say the magnetic field is CHANGING or alternating. Saying current passes through the core is wrong.",
      "A generator uses SLIP RINGS; a d.c. motor uses ONE SPLIT RING with connections inside the ring. Learn the difference — the terms are easily confused.",
      "Permanent magnets are made of STEEL. Iron is used for temporary magnets and transformer cores.",
      "Lenz's law: the induced effect OPPOSES the change causing it. Examiners report this as one of the hardest ideas on the paper."
    ],
    4: [
      "Say radiation is emitted FROM THE NUCLEUS. Many students describe the particle but never mention the nucleus.",
      "Always correct for background radiation first, then work with the corrected count, then add background back if the question asks for a counter reading.",
      "Know the difference between fission and fusion, and where each happens. Examiners list this as a recurring confusion.",
      "For chain reactions, give the full sequence: neutrons released are absorbed by other U-235 nuclei, which split and release more neutrons. Examiners say this was 'rarely seen'.",
      "The MODERATOR slows neutrons; CONTROL RODS absorb them. Mixing these up costs both marks.",
      "Balancing a BETA decay equation is harder than alpha — the −1 subscript on the beta particle matters.",
      "When reading half-life from a graph, plot the points carefully first. A common error was misreading the half-life from the axis spacing.",
      "Gamma radiation is uncharged, so it passes STRAIGHT through both electric and magnetic fields, undeflected."
    ],
    5: [
      "A light-year is a DISTANCE — the distance light travels in one year. Examiners report many students describing it as a time.",
      "Orbital speed uses the CIRCUMFERENCE of the orbit (2πr), not the distance from the Sun. This is the most common error in the whole topic.",
      "Orbital period increases with distance, but it is NOT proportional. Do not say doubling the distance doubles the period.",
      "The arrow for the force on an orbiting planet must point AT THE SUN. Examiners report most students drew it along a tangent, or left off the arrowhead.",
      "For redshift: the further the galaxy, the greater the redshift, so the faster it moves away — showing the Universe is expanding from a small, dense start.",
      "The Milky Way is about 100 000 light-years across. Few students knew this syllabus figure.",
      "The heaviest elements are formed in the SUPERNOVA stage. Red giant and protostar were popular wrong answers.",
      "The Sun becomes a red giant then a white dwarf. Only much more massive stars become supernovae, neutron stars or black holes."
    ]
  }
}
};

function examinerFor(subjectId, topicIndex) {
  const e = EXAMINER[subjectId];
  if (!e || !e.topicNotes || !e.topicNotes[topicIndex]) return null;
  return { source: e.source, notes: e.topicNotes[topicIndex] };
}

/* how many real questions are indexed for a subject/topic */
function realCount(subjectId, topicIndex) {
  try {
    const s = REAL_INDEX[subjectId];
    return (s && s[topicIndex]) ? s[topicIndex].length : 0;
  } catch (e) { return 0; }
}
/* total real questions indexed for a subject */
function realTotal(subjectId) {
  const s = REAL_INDEX[subjectId];
  if (!s) return 0;
  return Object.keys(s).reduce((n, k) => n + s[k].length, 0);
}
